<h2>Why the legislature set a clock</h2>
<p>A maintenance claim decided after years vindicates a right while defeating its purpose. Parliament's answer, first grafted onto Section 125 CrPC and now housed in the second proviso to Section 144(2) BNSS, is a disposal norm for the interim stage: sixty days from service. The interim order is meant to be provisional and quick precisely because it can later be adjusted — upward or downward — in the final order, and because the affidavit-based procedure gives the court enough material for a fair provisional figure without a trial.</p>

<h2>The machinery that makes sixty days feasible</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Filing with the affidavit.</strong> The claimant files the application with her affidavit of assets, income and expenditure in the prescribed format; incomplete affidavits invite exactly the adjournments the timeline is meant to prevent.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Service, proved fast.</strong> Summons go by ordinary modes and, with leave, by email and messaging; placing delivery reports on record fixes the date from which the sixty days run.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Respondent's affidavit.</strong> The respondent files his own affidavit of assets and liabilities within the time the court sets; the two affidavits, tested against documents, are the core material for the interim determination.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Interim order.</strong> The court fixes a monthly figure by reference to the standard of living, the incomes disclosed and the needs of the claimant and children — expressly subject to final adjustment.</div>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>The affidavit-of-assets regime is the engine of speed. A claimant who files a complete affidavit and immediately seeks directions for the respondent's affidavit converts the case from a pleading battle into a document comparison — which is a sixty-day task.</p></div>

<h2>When the timeline slips: the litigant's options</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Situation</th><th>Practical response</th></tr>
<tr><td>Respondent evades service</td><td>Seek substituted and electronic service promptly; evasion documented on record supports proceeding ex parte on the interim application.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Respondent withholds his affidavit of assets</td><td>Press for a peremptory direction; persistent default can justify striking the defence on the interim application and drawing adverse inferences on income.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeated adjournments beyond the norm</td><td>Invoke the sixty-day proviso on record and seek a short, fixed schedule; costs for avoidable adjournments can be sought.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Application pending long past the benchmark</td><td>A petition to the High Court under its supervisory jurisdiction seeking a time-bound disposal direction is an accepted remedy for stagnation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Urgent need before any order</td><td>Ask for ad interim directions — for example, continuation of household outgoings — pending the interim adjudication itself.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The respondent's perspective</h2>
<p>The timeline is not only the claimant's friend. A respondent facing an inflated claim benefits from an early, evidence-anchored interim order rather than months of uncertainty ending in a large arrear liability computed from the application date. Filing a candid affidavit quickly, with salary slips and bank statements, lets him contest quantum on his real numbers instead of the inferences courts draw against non-disclosers — inferences that tend to be generous to the claimant. And because interim orders are adjustable in the final order, the respondent loses little by early disposal and risks much by delay.</p>

<h2>The larger point</h2>
<p>Maintenance procedure has been progressively re-engineered around disclosure and speed: standardised asset affidavits, service by electronic means, interim awards from the application date, and the sixty-day disposal norm now restated in the BNSS. Litigants who work with that architecture — complete papers, proved service, insistence on the statutory rhythm — generally find that the system, for all its congestion, can be made to move at something close to the pace the statute intends.</p>
